Found in k74 (reported, not built, by decision). Lines are now marked when cut, but a reply has no byte cap: a run_* reply can reach about 2 MiB when the last 300 lines are long (the supervisor keeps the first and last MiB past 2 MiB); a logs line can be up to 1 MiB, so logs lines=50 could return 50 MiB, and the 500-line buffer can hold up to 500 MiB. A reply that large floods the agent context or the MCP client. Fix sketch: a byte cap per reply (and possibly per line) with the same "[... N bytes omitted]" marker style k74 introduced; decide the cap from what Claude Code accepts as tool output. Done when no reply exceeds the cap, the marker says what was cut, and tests pin it. Lane: unreagent-worker; after k74 lands.

Main agent's decision (2026-11-21): one byte cap per reply, 96 KiB, for run_* and logs replies. Claude Code's default MCP output limit is about 25,000 tokens, roughly 100 KB. Keep the newest output (the tail) and cut the oldest, with the k74 marker style naming the bytes omitted. No separate per-line cap: a single oversized line is cut at the reply cap with the marker. The cap is a named constant, not a config option.

